// Grader golden set. One JSON object per line; lines starting with // are ignored.
// label_status: "proposed" = drafted, NOT yet reviewed by a human. Review/relabel before trusting metrics.
// trap: verbatim | own_words | connect | compress | failure | vague | misconception | confident_wrong | beyond_source | grounded | off_task
{"id":"cs-hash-verbatim","trap":"verbatim","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consistent hashing","concept_definition":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Explain consistent hashing in your own words.","user_response":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":1}}
{"id":"cs-hash-own-words","trap":"own_words","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consistent hashing","concept_definition":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Explain consistent hashing in your own words.","user_response":"Picture a circle. You place servers at points on it and also place each key on it by hashing. A key belongs to the first server you hit going clockwise. If a server leaves, only the keys that were sitting just before it move to the next server; everything else stays put."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":2}}
{"id":"cs-hash-connect","trap":"connect","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consistent hashing","concept_definition":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it.","task_kind":"connection","task_prompt":"How does consistent hashing relate to other ideas you know?","user_response":"It's the fix for plain modulo sharding: with hash(key) mod N, changing N reshuffles almost every key, which is a cache stampede in a memcached fleet. The ring limits movement to about 1/N of keys. Virtual nodes then smooth out load imbalance, the same way you'd use many small partitions in Kafka to spread work."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":3}}
{"id":"cs-hash-failure","trap":"failure","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consistent hashing","concept_definition":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it.","task_kind":"misconception_resistance","task_prompt":"When does consistent hashing fail to balance load?","user_response":"Minimal remapping doesn't mean balanced load. With few nodes the arcs are uneven, so one server can own a huge slice; virtual nodes help. But even perfect arcs don't save you from hot keys: if one key gets 30% of traffic, it all lands on one node no matter how the ring looks. You need replication or key splitting for that. Also, when a node dies its whole arc goes to a single successor, which can cascade."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":5}}
{"id":"cs-hash-vague","trap":"vague","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consistent hashing","concept_definition":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Explain consistent hashing in your own words.","user_response":"It's a way of hashing that is consistent so things go to the right place in distributed systems."},"expected":{"score":"gap","stage":1}}
{"id":"cs-hash-misconception","trap":"misconception","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consistent hashing","concept_definition":"A hashing scheme that maps both keys and nodes onto a ring so that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys adjacent to it.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Explain consistent hashing in your own words.","user_response":"Consistent hashing guarantees every server gets exactly the same number of keys, which is why nothing needs to move when you add a server."},"expected":{"score":"misconception","stage":1}}
{"id":"bio-osmosis-verbatim","trap":"verbatim","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Osmosis","concept_definition":"The net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"What is osmosis?","user_response":"The net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":1}}
{"id":"bio-osmosis-confident-wrong","trap":"confident_wrong","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Osmosis","concept_definition":"The net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration.","task_kind":"application","task_prompt":"A red blood cell is placed in pure water. What happens and why?","user_response":"Definitely it shrinks. Salt moves out of the cell into the water by osmosis until both sides are equal, so the cell loses volume and crenates."},"expected":{"score":"misconception","stage":1}}
{"id":"bio-osmosis-apply","trap":"own_words","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Osmosis","concept_definition":"The net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration.","task_kind":"application","task_prompt":"A red blood cell is placed in pure water. What happens and why?","user_response":"Pure water has no solute, and the inside of the cell has lots, so water flows into the cell. The membrane keeps the solutes in, so the cell swells and can burst (hemolysis)."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":2}}
{"id":"bio-osmosis-connect","trap":"connect","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Osmosis","concept_definition":"The net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration.","task_kind":"connection","task_prompt":"Connect osmosis to another biological process.","user_response":"Plant cells rely on osmosis for turgor pressure: water flows into the vacuole, pushing the membrane against the cell wall, which keeps leaves rigid. It's the same driving force as hemolysis in red blood cells, but the wall stops the cell from bursting. Kidneys exploit it too, concentrating urine by building a salt gradient in the medulla."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":3}}
{"id":"econ-oppcost-compress","trap":"compress","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Opportunity cost","concept_definition":"The value of the next-best alternative given up when a choice is made.","task_kind":"compression","task_prompt":"State opportunity cost as briefly and fundamentally as you can.","user_response":"Every choice is priced in the best thing you didn't choose, because resources used one way can't be used another."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":4}}
{"id":"econ-oppcost-sunk","trap":"misconception","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Opportunity cost","concept_definition":"The value of the next-best alternative given up when a choice is made.","task_kind":"application","task_prompt":"You paid $50 for a concert ticket but feel sick. What is the opportunity cost of going?","user_response":"The opportunity cost is the $50 you spent on the ticket, so you should go so you don't waste it."},"expected":{"score":"misconception","stage":1}}
{"id":"econ-oppcost-own","trap":"own_words","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Opportunity cost","concept_definition":"The value of the next-best alternative given up when a choice is made.","task_kind":"application","task_prompt":"You paid $50 for a concert ticket but feel sick. What is the opportunity cost of going?","user_response":"The $50 is gone either way, so it doesn't count. The cost of going is what you'd do instead, like resting at home and recovering faster."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":2}}
{"id":"stats-pvalue-misconception","trap":"misconception","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"p-value","concept_definition":"The probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a result at least as extreme as the one observed.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"What does p = 0.03 mean?","user_response":"There is a 3% chance that the null hypothesis is true."},"expected":{"score":"misconception","stage":1}}
{"id":"stats-pvalue-own","trap":"own_words","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"p-value","concept_definition":"The probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a result at least as extreme as the one observed.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"What does p = 0.03 mean?","user_response":"If there really were no effect, you'd see data this extreme or more extreme only about 3% of the time."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":2}}
{"id":"stats-pvalue-failure","trap":"failure","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"p-value","concept_definition":"The probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of observing a result at least as extreme as the one observed.","task_kind":"misconception_resistance","task_prompt":"When can a small p-value mislead you?","user_response":"A p-value says how surprising the data is under the null, not how big or real the effect is. With a huge sample, trivial effects get tiny p-values. If you run 20 tests, one will hit p<0.05 by chance, so multiple comparisons and p-hacking inflate false positives. And it's conditional on the null, so it's not the probability the null is true; that needs a prior."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":5}}
{"id":"law-consideration-wrong","trap":"misconception","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consideration","concept_definition":"Something of legal value bargained for and exchanged between parties to a contract.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"What is consideration in contract law?","user_response":"It's when both parties consider the contract carefully before signing it."},"expected":{"score":"misconception","stage":0}}
{"id":"law-consideration-own","trap":"own_words","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Consideration","concept_definition":"Something of legal value bargained for and exchanged between parties to a contract.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"What is consideration in contract law?","user_response":"Each side has to give something of value in exchange for the other's promise. A promise to give a gift with nothing in return usually isn't enforceable because there's no bargained-for exchange."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":2}}
{"id":"chem-lechatelier-partial","trap":"vague","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Le Chatelier's principle","concept_definition":"When a system at equilibrium is disturbed, the equilibrium shifts in the direction that counteracts the disturbance.","task_kind":"application","task_prompt":"For N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 (exothermic), what happens to NH3 yield if you raise the temperature and if you raise the pressure?","user_response":"Raising the pressure pushes it toward NH3 because there are fewer gas molecules on that side. Temperature also increases the yield because reactions go faster when it's hotter."},"expected":{"score":"gap","stage":2}}
{"id":"ground-tcp-grounded","trap":"grounded","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"TCP three-way handshake","concept_definition":"The SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK exchange that establishes a TCP connection.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Describe the TCP three-way handshake.","user_response":"The client sends SYN with its initial sequence number, the server answers SYN-ACK acknowledging it and sending its own sequence number, and the client sends ACK. Then both sides have agreed on sequence numbers and data can flow.","source_context":"Definition: The SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK exchange that establishes a TCP connection.\nQuote (p.212): The client begins by sending a SYN segment carrying its initial sequence number. The server replies with a SYN-ACK that acknowledges the client's sequence number and carries the server's own initial sequence number. Finally the client sends an ACK, after which both ends have synchronized sequence numbers and the connection is established."},"expected":{"score":"recognizes","stage":2,"expects_unsupported":false}}
{"id":"ground-tcp-beyond","trap":"beyond_source","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"TCP three-way handshake","concept_definition":"The SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK exchange that establishes a TCP connection.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Describe the TCP three-way handshake.","user_response":"Client sends SYN, server sends SYN-ACK, client sends ACK. It exists mainly to defend against SYN flood attacks, and TCP Fast Open lets the client send data in the very first SYN to skip a round trip.","source_context":"Definition: The SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK exchange that establishes a TCP connection.\nQuote (p.212): The client begins by sending a SYN segment carrying its initial sequence number. The server replies with a SYN-ACK that acknowledges the client's sequence number and carries the server's own initial sequence number. Finally the client sends an ACK, after which both ends have synchronized sequence numbers and the connection is established."},"expected":{"score":"misconception","stage":1,"expects_unsupported":true}}
{"id":"ground-photosynth-beyond","trap":"beyond_source","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Light-dependent reactions","concept_definition":"The stage of photosynthesis in the thylakoid membranes that converts light energy into ATP and NADPH, releasing O2 from water.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"Explain the light-dependent reactions.","user_response":"In the thylakoids, light splits water, releasing oxygen, and the energy is captured as ATP and NADPH for the Calvin cycle. The process is about 30% efficient and evolved roughly 2.4 billion years ago during the Great Oxidation Event.","source_context":"Definition: The stage of photosynthesis in the thylakoid membranes that converts light energy into ATP and NADPH, releasing O2 from water.\nQuote (p.88): Light absorbed by photosystem II drives the splitting of water, releasing O2. Electrons flow through the transport chain to photosystem I, producing ATP by chemiosmosis and reducing NADP+ to NADPH. These products power the Calvin cycle in the stroma."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":3,"expects_unsupported":true}}
{"id":"ground-photosynth-grounded","trap":"grounded","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Light-dependent reactions","concept_definition":"The stage of photosynthesis in the thylakoid membranes that converts light energy into ATP and NADPH, releasing O2 from water.","task_kind":"connection","task_prompt":"How do the light-dependent reactions connect to the rest of photosynthesis?","user_response":"Photosystem II uses light to split water, which is where the O2 comes from. Electrons move down the chain to photosystem I; along the way ATP is made by chemiosmosis and NADP+ becomes NADPH. Those two products are exactly what the Calvin cycle in the stroma spends to fix carbon, so the light reactions are the power supply for the dark reactions.","source_context":"Definition: The stage of photosynthesis in the thylakoid membranes that converts light energy into ATP and NADPH, releasing O2 from water.\nQuote (p.88): Light absorbed by photosystem II drives the splitting of water, releasing O2. Electrons flow through the transport chain to photosystem I, producing ATP by chemiosmosis and reducing NADP+ to NADPH. These products power the Calvin cycle in the stroma."},"expected":{"score":"understood","stage":3,"expects_unsupported":false}}
{"id":"hist-offtask","trap":"off_task","label_status":"proposed","input":{"concept_name":"Treaty of Westphalia","concept_definition":"The 1648 peace settlements ending the Thirty Years' War, often credited with establishing the principle of state sovereignty.","task_kind":"definition","task_prompt":"What is the significance of the Treaty of Westphalia?","user_response":"I don't remember this one."},"expected":{"score":"gap","stage":0}}
